- helm

' Publication between two worlds, as pure rules over the namespace.
'
' A world publishes the names below a root record, /<self>/our/<peer>. The
' reader holds them under the same names, in the store it keeps for the
' publisher (namespace.peers). That store is not part of the reader's own
' names. What the reader knows about it is in the reader's own records: where
' each root stands, and what the publisher said of each name.
'
' No rule here does I/O. A foot carries the epics these rules make and asks
' the world to land them as one admission (helm/card/land).
'
' No rule here holds a key or checks a signature. Mesa signs each page a
' world publishes and checks each page from a peer. A change of key changes
' no name and no rule.
+ net
  + segment_is
    ' True if two path segments spell the same word. It compares the spelling,
    ' because the runtime faults when a symbol and a numeric segment are
    ' compared as iotas.
    \ a=iota b=iota
    ^ bool
    | equal[str] (path_show [a]) (path_show [b])
  + stamp
    ' The version a world publishes: its rift and its case as one number. A
    ' restore raises the rift, so a world never uses a published version twice,
    ' and a newer continuity always compares greater than an older one. A
    ' reference pinned to a stamp names one content for good.
    \ rift=nat case=nat
    ^ nat
    | add (mul rift 4294967296) case
  + entry
    ' One name of an epic:
    ' - its path below the root
    ' - the version of its record
    ' - the content hash of the record (0: the name is gone)
    ' - the record
    '
    ' An epic travels as its entries without their records. Each record follows
    ' by its hash. The publisher does not send a record the reader already has.
    : rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat record=maybe[myth]
  + put
    ' An entry for a record.
    \ rel=path stamp=nat record=myth
    ^ net/entry
    | net/entry rel stamp (net/content record) (./some record)
  + drop
    ' An entry for a name that is gone.
    \ rel=path
    ^ net/entry
    = absent=maybe[myth] .none
    | net/entry rel 0 0 absent
  + epic
    ' One consistent change of a published root, from version base (0: from
    ' nothing) to version head. An epic travels as one page. Mesa signs that
    ' page for the publisher and checks it for the reader: the names, the
    ' removals, the base and the head.
    : base=nat head=nat entries=row[net/entry]
  + sent
    ' A record as its publisher sends it. A pinned reference to one of the
    ' publisher's names carries the published version (net/stamp) in place of
    ' the local case. A local case means nothing in another world.
    \ self=iota rift=nat record=myth
    ^ myth
    | record.tap.fold record
      \ out [slot value]
      ? value
       > target=safe
        ? (eq target.ver 0) out
        ? (eq target.path.len 0) out
        ? !(net/segment_is target.path.at(0) self) out
        | out.put(slot (safe (net/stamp rift target.ver) target.care target.path))
      out
  + puts
    ' The names in after that are new or changed since before.
    \ self=iota rift=nat before=axal[saga] after=axal[saga]
    ^ row[net/entry]
    = none=row[net/entry] []
    | after.tap.fold none
      \ out [rel now]
      = case now.aeon.yuga.x.data
        same
          ?> (./some then) before.get(rel) .false
          | eq then.aeon.yuga.x.data case
      ? same out
      | out.snoc((net/put rel (net/stamp rift case) (net/sent self rift (unpin now.myth))))
  + gone
    ' The names in before that after no longer has.
    \ before=axal[saga] after=axal[saga]
    ^ row[net/entry]
    = none=row[net/entry] []
    | before.tap.fold none
      \ out [rel then]
      ? (maybe/is_some after.get(rel)) out
      | out.snoc((net/drop rel))
  + delta
    ' What changed below a published root between its z cases k and n. k = 0
    ' asks for everything below the root. If it cannot answer case k or case n
    ' exactly, it gives none and the reader asks for everything. It never gives
    ' a wrong or partial answer.
    \ rift=nat root=path k=nat n=nat state=namespace
    ^ maybe[net/epic]
    ? !(lt k n) .none
    = history state.local
      empty (axal/new[saga] .none)
    ?> (./some now) (namespace/epoch .z (./some n) root history) .none
    = after | namespace/records now root history
    ? (eq k 0)
      | ./some (net/epic 0 (net/stamp rift n) (net/puts state.self rift empty after))
    ?> (./some then) (namespace/epoch .z (./some k) root history) .none
    = before | namespace/records then root history
      changed | net/puts state.self rift before after
    | ./some (net/epic (net/stamp rift k) (net/stamp rift n) changed.weld((net/gone before after)))
  + content
    ' The content hash of a record: the hash of its pin.
    \ record=myth
    ^ nat
    | PinHash (pin record)
  + fits
    ' True if every record of an epic is the content the epic names for it, and
    ' no name that is gone has a record.
    \ epic=net/epic
    ^ bool
    | epic.entries.all
      \ item=net/entry
      ^ bool
      ? (eq 0 item.hash) (maybe/is_none item.record)
      ?> (./some record) item.record .true
      | eq item.hash (net/content record)
  + note
    ' Where this world keeps what a publisher said of one of the publisher's
    ' names. The name /<publisher>/... has its statement at
    ' /<self>/io/mesa/<publisher>/said/...
    '
    ' A statement is a record of this world. The record it is about is in the
    ' store kept for the publisher, not in this world. Each has its own cases.
    \ self=iota name=path
    ^ path
    = head=path [self 'io 'mesa name.at(0) 'said]
    | head.weld(name.drop(1))
  + stating
    ' The record of a statement: the publisher's version and the content hash
    ' of the record at that version. A hash of 0 means the publisher no longer
    ' has the name.
    \ stamp=nat hash=nat
    ^ myth
    | (myth/sing ['stamp] (pails/n stamp)).put(['hash] (pails/n hash))
  + left
    ' True if this world holds the statement that a publisher's name is gone:
    ' it held a record and now holds none.
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ bool
    ?> (./some told) (sovereign_care .x (net/note state.self name) state).fil .false
    | eq 0 told.nat_at(['hash] 0)
  + held
    ' A number kept on a record, or 0 when there is none.
    \ slot=path name=path state=namespace
    ^ nat
    | (sovereign_care .x name state).fil.fall(myth/new).nat_at(slot 0)
  + forget
    ' Adds the write that says a publisher's name is gone at a head. Adds
    ' nothing when this world holds no statement for the name.
    \ target=path head=nat state=namespace out=row[[p=path q=writ]]
    ^ row[[p=path q=writ]]
    ? (eq 0 (net/version target state)) out
    | out.snoc([(net/note state.self target) (writ/make (net/stating head 0))])
  + version
    ' The publisher's version that a held record stands at. 0 when this world
    ' holds no statement for the name, or the statement says the name is gone.
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ nat
    = at=path (net/note state.self name)
    ? (eq 0 (net/held ['hash] at state)) 0
    | net/held ['stamp] at state
  + live
    \ name=path state=namespace
    ^ bool
    | maybe/is_some (sovereign_care .x name state).fil
  + above
    ' Applies a kernel law: no record may live below a name that held a record
    ' and now holds none. Within one continuity the publisher obeys the same
    ' law, so this does nothing. After a restore the publisher's history is a
    ' different one. If it lands below a name where it has no record and the
    ' store removed one, this makes that name again, empty.
    \ root=path rel=path made=axal[myth] state=namespace out=axal[note]
    ^ axal[note]
    % k=nat 0
      acc=axal[note] out
    ^ axal[note]
    ? (ge k rel.len) acc
    = name=path rel.take(k)
      target=path root.weld(name)
    ? (maybe/is_some made.dip(name).fil) (_ k.inc acc)
    ? (net/live target state) (_ k.inc acc)
    ? !(net/left target state) (_ k.inc acc)
    ? (maybe/is_some acc.get(target)) (_ k.inc acc)
    | _ k.inc acc.put(target (note/make myth/new))
  + landing
    ' The plan of one landing:
    ' - the epoch of the publisher's store that the plan was made on
    ' - the changes to that store: the publisher's names, each with exactly the
    '   content the publisher sent
    ' - this world's own writes that go with them: what the publisher said of
    '   each name (net/note), and where the root then stands
    '
    ' The two parts are one admission (helm/card/land).
    : base=nat diffs=axal[note] writes=row[[p=path q=writ]]
  + land
    ' Plans the landing of one epic of a publisher's root. Each record lands
    ' under the name the publisher gave it, in the store kept for that
    ' publisher, with exactly the content the publisher sent. A case in that
    ' store counts what this world received. The publisher's version goes in a
    ' record of this world (net/note). No behaviour of a landed record runs:
    ' the kernel runs behaviours for this world's own names only.
    '
    ' It does not decide who the publisher is. The foot hands it an epic, and
    ' the foot only gets an epic as a page Mesa checked under the publisher's
    ' name. The card is where this world keeps where the root stands.
    '
    ' It refuses the whole epic when:
    ' - the epic is not newer than where the root stands (net_stale)
    ' - the epic does not start where the root stands (net_gap)
    ' - a record is not the content the epic names for it (net_unfit)
    '
    ' It does not change a record the store already holds at that version or a
    ' later one, and does not remove a name that is already gone. So no entry
    ' lands twice and no version goes back. An epic of everything below the
    ' root also removes what the publisher no longer has.
    \ root=path card=path epic=net/epic state=namespace
    ^ either[iota net/landing]
    = have | net/held ['head] card state
    ? (le epic.head have) (./err 'net_stale)
    ? !((eq epic.base 0) || (eq epic.base have)) (./err 'net_gap)
    ? (eq 0 root.len) (./err 'net_outside)
    ? (state.owns(root)) (./err 'net_outside)
    ? !(net/fits epic) (./err 'net_unfit)
    ? !(net/whole root epic state) (./err 'net_missing)
    = none=row[[p=path q=writ]] []
      whole (eq epic.base 0)
      peer=path [root.at(0)]
      base
        ?> (./some store) state.peers.get(peer) 0
        store.top
      made
        | epic.entries.fold (axal/new[myth] .none)
          \ tree=axal[myth] item=net/entry
          ^ axal[myth]
          ? (eq 0 item.hash) tree
          | tree.put(item.rel myth/new)
      moved
        | epic.entries.fold (net/landing base (axal/new[note] .none) none)
          \ plan=net/landing item=net/entry
          ^ net/landing
          = target=path root.weld(item.rel)
          ? (eq 0 item.hash)
            ? !(net/live target state) plan
            | plan.set_diffs(plan.diffs.put(target .cull)).set_writes((net/forget target epic.head state plan.writes))
          ? (le item.stamp (net/version target state)) plan
          ?> (./some record) item.record plan
          = said=writ (writ/make (net/stating item.stamp item.hash))
          | plan.set_diffs(plan.diffs.put(target (note/make record))).set_writes(plan.writes.snoc([(net/note state.self target) said]))
      stale
        ? !whole moved
        | (sovereign_peek root state).tap.fold moved
          \ plan=net/landing [rel record]
          ^ net/landing
          = under made.dip(rel)
          ? (maybe/is_some under.fil) plan
          = target=path root.weld(rel)
            gone (plan.set_writes((net/forget target epic.head state plan.writes)))
          ? (gt under.kid.size 0) gone.set_diffs(gone.diffs.put(target (note/make myth/new)))
          | gone.set_diffs(gone.diffs.put(target .cull))
      bare
        ? !whole stale.diffs
        | epic.entries.fold stale.diffs
          \ out=axal[note] item=net/entry
          ^ axal[note]
          ? (eq 0 item.hash) out
          | net/above root item.rel made state out
      mark=myth (myth/sing ['head] (pails/n epic.head))
      stands=writ (? (net/live card state) (writ/poke mark) (writ/make mark))
    | ./ok (net/landing base bare stale.writes.snoc([card stands]))
  + lacks
    ' The content hashes of the records this world still has to get for an
    ' epic: the ones newer than what it holds of the root.
    \ root=path epic=net/epic state=namespace
    ^ row[nat]
    = none=row[nat] []
    | epic.entries.fold none
      \ out=row[nat] item=net/entry
      ^ row[nat]
      ? (eq 0 item.hash) out
      ? (maybe/is_some item.record) out
      ? (le item.stamp (net/version root.weld(item.rel) state)) out
      ? (out.any((\ seen=nat (eq seen item.hash)))) out
      | out.snoc(item.hash)
  + whole
    ' True if every record an epic will land has arrived.
    \ root=path epic=net/epic state=namespace
    ^ bool
    | eq 0 (net/lacks root epic state).len
  + refuse
    ' The write that records a refused landing. It keeps the head and the
    ' reason beside where the root stands. What this world holds of the root is
    ' untouched, and a later epic lands from where the root stands.
    \ card=path head=nat why=iota state=namespace
    ^ [p=path q=writ]
    = note=myth (myth/sing ['refused] (pails/n head)).put(['why] (pails/q why))
    ? (net/live card state) [card (writ/poke note)]
    [card (writ/make note)]
  + wants
    ' True if the reader should ask for a head that a publisher gave in a hint.
    ' It asks only for a head later than what it holds and later than what it
    ' refused. A hint is compared, never believed.
    \ card=path head=nat state=namespace
    ^ bool
    ? (le head (net/held ['head] card state)) .false
    | gt head (net/held ['refused] card state)
  + hear
    ' How the foot learns that the world has something new to publish. The node
    ' /<self>/our sits above everything this world publishes, and its crew
    ' names that subtree. When the subtree changes, its hand counts on the
    ' record of the foot's node (`tide`). The kernel then tells the foot, as it
    ' tells any foot when its own record changes.
    '
    ' This hand is not on the foot's node. The kernel wakes no node below
    ' /<self>/io: that subtree is for feet.
    \ b=bowl cur=myth changed=row[dep]
    ^ yell
    = none=row[[p=path q=writ]] []
      self b.here.at(0)
      home=path [self 'io 'mesa]
    ? !(changed.any((\ d=dep (pith_prefix [self 'our] d.p)))) (yell none cur)
    = seen
        ?> (./some tree) b.crew.get(['foot]) 0
        | tree.fil.fall(myth/new).nat_at(['tide] 0)
      told=row[[p=path q=writ]] [[home (writ/poke (myth/sing ['tide] (pails/n seen.inc)))]]
    | yell told cur
  + herald
    ' The hand of /<self>/our.
    ^ form
    def_form.set_hear(net/hear)
  + watch
    ' What /<self>/our must hold to have its hand: the behaviour, and the crew
    ' the hand hears through.
    \ self=iota
    ^ myth
    = ours=path [self 'our]
      home=path [self 'io 'mesa]
      crew (crew/sing ['our] (dep .z ours)).put(['foot] (dep .x home))
    | (myth/sing limb_slot (kook [(net/horn self)])).put(crew_slot (cpail crew))
  + horn
    ' The name of the record that holds the hand of /<self>/our.
    \ self=iota
    ^ path
    [self 'kook 'herald]
  + given
    ' An epic with one arrived record put in. The record goes where the epic
    ' names its hash.
    \ hash=nat record=myth epic=net/epic
    ^ net/epic
    | epic.set_entries
      | epic.entries.map
        \ item=net/entry
        ^ net/entry
        ? !(eq item.hash hash) item
        | item.set_record((./some record))
  + wired
    ' An epic as it travels: plain rows and no records, so every kernel makes
    ' the same bytes. Each line is one name: its path, version and content
    ' hash.
    \ epic=net/epic
    ^ [base=nat head=nat lines=row[[rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat]]]
    = lines
        | epic.entries.map
          \ item=net/entry
          ^ [rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat]
          [item.rel item.stamp item.hash]
    [epic.base epic.head lines]
  + read
    ' An epic from a value that arrived. It assumes nothing about the value and
    ' gives none unless the value has exactly the travelling form, to the last
    ' nat.
    \ value=any
    ^ maybe[net/epic]
    = none=maybe[net/epic] .none
      absent=maybe[myth] .none
    ?> (./some sent) (fit[[base=nat head=nat lines=row[[rel=path stamp=nat hash=nat]]]] value) none
    = [base head lines] sent
      entries
        | lines.map
          \ [rel stamp hash]
          ^ net/entry
          | net/entry rel stamp hash absent
    | ./some (net/epic base head entries)
  + shaped
    ' The number of slots of a well-formed myth whose slots all lie between two
    ' bounds. None when the kernel could not have made the tree: a slot is out
    ' of order, or a count is not the right sum.
    \ lo=maybe[path] hi=maybe[path] tree=mop[path pail lt_pith]
    ^ maybe[nat]
    > n=mop/node[path pail lt_pith] tree
     | ./some 0
    = none=maybe[nat] .none
      mid=maybe[path] (./some n.key)
      below
        ?> (./some bound) lo .true
        | lt_pith bound n.key
      above
        ?> (./some bound) hi .true
        | lt_pith n.key bound
    ? !(and below above) none
    ?> (./some left) (net/shaped lo mid n.l) none
    ?> (./some right) (net/shaped mid hi n.r) none
    ? !(eq n.sz left.inc.add(right)) none
    | ./some n.sz
  + sound
    ' A record from a value that arrived. It assumes nothing about the value:
    ' - it checks the representation at every depth
    ' - the slots must be in order
    ' - a slot the kernel reads as a reference must be a whole reference
    '
    ' Whoever reads any other slot must check what it holds. No arrived value
    ' holds code (the runtime refuses a stream with a law in it).
    \ value=any
    ^ maybe[myth]
    = none=maybe[myth] .none
      open=maybe[path] .none
    ?> (./some record) (fit[myth] value) none
    ? (maybe/is_none (net/shaped open open record)) none
    = whole
        | record.tap.all
          \ entry
          ?> target=safe entry.val .true
          | maybe/is_some (fit[safe] entry.val)
    ? whole (./some record)
    none
